@meta
id: 31-13
title: Eight claims, true or false
chapter: 31
type: lesson
skills: nvim-differences, nvim-lsp, treesitter
par: 84
minutes: 9
vscode: no equivalent — this is the argument you will have with a colleague
open: claims.txt

@brief
Every one of these is something you will hear said about Neovim, usually
with confidence. Four are true and four are not. Work out which, from what
the chapter has covered, and write `true` or `false` after each `=`.

Read them carefully — several are true of something adjacent to what they
claim, which is exactly how the confusion spreads.

Graded in vim. Nothing runs; the judgement is the exercise.

@keys
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `j` | down a line; `A` ignores the column |

@hints
Two of the false ones are about things Neovim deliberately did not do, and
two are about things people credit it with that come from somewhere else.
--
Ask of each claim: is this about the editing language, the configuration,
or the tooling? Neovim changed the last two and left the first alone.
--
`A`, the answer, `Esc`, `j` — eight times down the file.

@solution
    /macros<Enter> Atrue<Esc> jAtrue<Esc> jAfalse<Esc> jAfalse<Esc>
    jAfalse<Esc> jAfalse<Esc> jAtrue<Esc> jAfalse<Esc> :wq<Enter>

Line by line:

1. **Macros and registers are identical.** True, and so is every motion,
   operator, text object, mark and ex command. Say it once more until it
   stops needing saying.
2. **Your vimscript still runs.** True. Neovim kept the vimscript
   interpreter; `vim.cmd` runs it from Lua, and an `init.vim` runs a vimrc
   nearly unchanged. The parts that break are the ones that poke at vim
   internals: `if has('python3')`, some `+clientserver` tricks, and
   anything using Vim9script.
3. **Both config files at once.** False. Neovim stops with a conflict
   error rather than choosing. One or the other.
4. **Lua is required.** False, and this matters if you are nervous about
   the migration. `init.vim` is a first-class path, and plenty of people
   run Neovim for years on a ported vimrc. Lua is how you get the
   ecosystem, not how you get the editor.
5. **Neovim ships language servers.** False. It ships the *client*. The
   server is a separate program you install yourself — `tsserver` via npm,
   `gopls` via go, and so on. `mason.nvim` exists because this step
   annoys everybody.
6. **Treesitter replaces LSP.** False, and they are not even about the
   same thing. Treesitter parses the buffer in front of you, which gets
   you highlighting, folds and structural text objects. LSP asks a program
   that understands your whole project, which gets you types, rename and
   diagnostics. You want both.
7. **A distro is someone else's config.** True, and that is the honest
   frame for LazyVim, AstroNvim and NvChad. They are excellent, they are
   thousands of lines you did not write, and the first time one breaks you
   will be debugging a config you have never read. The alternative worth
   knowing is `kickstart.nvim`: a single commented `init.lua`, about 400
   lines, meant to be read and then owned. If you have written your own
   vimrc — and this dojo made you do that in chapter 28 — start there
   instead of with a framework.
8. **Vim9script plugins run in Neovim.** False. Vim 9 introduced a new,
   faster scripting language; Neovim declined to implement it and went
   further with Lua. This is the real fork in the road, and it is why a
   plugin written in 2024 may work in exactly one of the two editors.

@start claims.txt
# Neovim, true or false.
# Put your answer right after the = with no spaces.

macros_and_registers_work_identically=
vimscript_from_your_vimrc_still_runs=
init_vim_and_init_lua_can_both_exist=
lua_is_required_to_configure_neovim=
neovim_ships_the_language_servers=
treesitter_replaces_lsp=
a_distro_is_someone_elses_config=
vim9script_plugins_run_in_neovim=

@expected claims.txt
# Neovim, true or false.
# Put your answer right after the = with no spaces.

macros_and_registers_work_identically=true
vimscript_from_your_vimrc_still_runs=true
init_vim_and_init_lua_can_both_exist=false
lua_is_required_to_configure_neovim=false
neovim_ships_the_language_servers=false
treesitter_replaces_lsp=false
